WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:05.140 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  it was the most chaotic we get in  Parliament's continuing failure to come  to grips with the public's vote to leave  the EU so the noes have it  the noes have it i profoundly regret the  decision that this house has taken  tonight the Prime Minister's run down  the clock and the clock has been run out  on her MPs voted against the Prime  Minister's deal then voted against  leaving without a deal before voting to  delay brexit I've been reporting from  this place for more than 30 years and  I've never seen anything like it  our nation is facing its most important  decision for half a century and yet it  seems government and Parliament are  unable or incapable of making up their  minds what to do about it much to the  annoyance of the people politics is in  meltdown of course Europe has always  divided politicians and parties like  nothing else in the 1970s the Labour  Prime Minister Harold Wilson got himself  out of a tricky corner by handing the  decision of whether to stay in the  European community to the public with a  referendum David Cameron tried the same  thing with less certainty and lost I do  not think it would be right for me to  try to be the captain that steers our  country to its next destination Theresa  May took on the job and decided to try  and take the country out of the EU with  as little input from MPs as possible as  we leave the European Union we will  forge a bold new positive role for  ourselves in the world but she was  thwarted by the Supreme Court which  ruled against the government and in  favour of a businesswoman named Gina  Miller no government can expect to be  unanswerable or unchallenged Parliament  alone is sovereign  MPs now had the right to approve the  deal to leave but they couldn't agree on  what deal they wanted the Prime Minister  called an election in the hope of  strengthening her hand but she lost her  majority in a hung parliament she had to  strike a deal with the DUP to stay in  power  but the Northern Irish unionists haven't  backed the government on brexit nor have  a significant number of Conservative MPs  mainly associated with the European  research group led by Jacob Riis MOG if  we went ahead and held the votes  tomorrow the deal would be rejected  missus may put off the moment of truth  by repeatedly delaying a meaningful vote  the parliamentary party does have  confidence and she had to fight off an  attempt by some of her own MPs to kick  her out in a vote of no confidence in  her leadership with the clock counting  down to brexit day mrs. Mae had to call  the meaningful vote the eyes to the  right 202 the nose to the left  432 she lost by 230 the biggest defeat  inflicted by MPs for at least a hundred  years having failed to sacker  conservative MPs then backed her in a  commons confidence vote to avoid having  a general election and then mrs. Mae  lost again by 149 this time nose to the  left 391 so the noes have it  the noes have it I love but she's still  here and is planning to ask MPs tobacker  in yet another meaningful vote next week  this is not how British politics used to  work when a prime minister couldn't get  laws passed let alone deliver a central  piece of their policy agenda they packed  their bags and resigned but these are  not normal times there's no precedent  for what's happening now back in the  1970s sonny jim callaghan's government  fell six days after losing a series of  votes in the Commons because labour no  longer had a majority Callaghan lost the  vote of no-confidence by one vote and a  general election followed good morning  prime minister a sad day in 1990  Conservative MPs were able to oust  Margaret Thatcher as party leader and  Prime  stur John major followed and was  defeated over Europe this time by his  own Euroskeptic rebels but he was able  to face them down rule changes since  then prevent mrs. maid from taking the  same way out  she can't resign the leadership and  stand again in spite of enduring the  largest rebellion ever by conservative  MPs nor can she call a snap election  because the fixed term Parliament act  now applies no Prime Minister at odds  with their own MPs has ever had fewer  options this is the time for this house  not just this government or indeed this  Prime Minister but for this house to  give a lead even when 139 Labour MPs  rebelled over Iraq Tony Blair had enough  support across the Commons to win the  vote outright David Cameron was forced  to rule out taking military action in  Syria after MPs voted to reject his  plans his authority was greatly  diminished but he left it at that  Theresa May can't walk away on brexit  two and a half years on all eyes are on  Westminster because nobody knows where  we're heading there's still no sign of  agreement instead it seems likely that  negotiations will be extended and the  fundamental problems remain the same  Teresa may's plan is unpopular her party  is divided so is Parliament and the  country these divisions have broken the  rules that govern us until and unless  that can be resolved the big question  mark there hang over whether our  political system is fit for the  challenges we face Adam Bolton Sky News  Westminster

